{{!-- RELATIONSHIP MAP — who at this table knows whom, and how.

      One window per map (there are several named maps in a world), driven by
      module/dialogs/RelationshipMapWindow.js. The board inside it pans and zooms as a whole; the
      strip above it and the footer below it do not ride the board, so the controls stay put while
      the web moves behind them.

      ⚠ THERE IS NO TOOLBAR OVER THE BOARD ANY MORE, and this is the shape of the window rather
      than a detail of it. A row of buttons stood between the window's title and the page strip,
      and by the end it held three things: "Match answers to people", which is gone outright; "Add
      someone"; and the undo/redo pair. All that is left of it now lives in the FOOTER under the
      board, on the same line as the box that turns the captions off — so the whole top of the
      window is what a reader came for (which board is up, and the board), and everything that is
      pressed at it sits in one place at the bottom. The row it replaced was measured over the edge
      at 1366px wide when it still carried a map name; three controls in the footer are not.

      ONE root element, as AppV1 requires. --}}
{{!-- ⚠ `labels-all` IS A CONSTANT AND NOT A SETTING. The window used to carry a three-way cycle for
      it (all / on hover / off) and the class was written from wherever that stood; the cycle is
      gone, and every line's caption is drawn. The class stays because the stylesheet's caption
      rules hang off it, and because a board too dense to caption still has the other way down:
      resting on a face lights that person's web and quiets the rest.

      `captions-off` IS THE ONE THE READER MOVES: the checkbox under the board, written here so a
      re-render does not put the words back under somebody who turned them off, and written again
      by `_paintChrome` so a repaint does not either. Two classes and not one, because the other
      way to a board with no writing on it -- zoomed out past the size at which words are worth
      drawing -- is the board's own decision rather than the reader's, and the two must be able to
      end without ending each other. See `_captionsHidden`. --}}
{{!-- ⚠ AND THE THREE WEIGHTS RIDE ON THE ROOT AS CUSTOM PROPERTIES, printed here rather than
      written on afterwards. `activateListeners` measures the captions the moment this markup lands
      (`_fitGapsToPaint` reads a real one's computed size straight off the document), so a weight
      that arrived a tick later would leave every sentence on the board cut, and every hole in every
      stroke opened, for writing of a size it is no longer set in. `_paintWeights` is the writer for
      every press after this one. See module/relmap/relmap-weights.js. --}}
<div class="stonetop-relmap labels-all{{#if hideLabels}} captions-off{{/if}}{{#unless canEdit}} is-readonly{{/unless}}"
     style="{{weightStyle}}">

  {{!-- WHICH BOARD OF THIS MAP IS UP.

        One map is several named boards, and each is a JournalEntryPage of the same entry carrying
        its own people, its own lines and its own layout (module/relmap/relmap-doc.js). A page is
        not another arrangement of one web: adding somebody to "The Millers" does not put them on
        "Marshedge", and the same person may stand on both, in different places, with different
        lines drawn to them. That is what actually thins out a village of forty.

        ITS OWN ROW, AND NOW THE FIRST ONE. It never belonged among the tools — a tool says what
        can be done to a board, and this says WHICH BOARD that is, which is the frame all of that
        happens inside — and with the toolbar gone (see the head of this file) the frame is what the
        window opens with, immediately over the board it names. The tools themselves are in the
        footer.

        A TAB STRIP AND NOT A DROPDOWN, which is the one place this window deliberately spends the
        height. Pages are a thing a table has to KNOW it can make, and a `<select>` showing one
        value tells nobody that a second is possible; a row of tabs with a "+" on the end is the
        whole feature, visible. It is also where a reader's eye already goes to ask "which one of
        these am I looking at".

        ⚠ THE TABS ARE DROPPED IN WHOLE, as the board and the person chooser are, and are built in
        `_pageTabs` — never written out here as an `{{#each}}`. Somebody at the far end of the table
        adds and renames these under an open window, and the repaint that keeps up with that can
        only reach the DOM. One builder, two writers; see `_paintPages`.

        The strip is ONE tab stop with the arrow keys inside it (a roving tabindex, written by the
        same builder), because a map with eight boards is otherwise eight stops between the window
        and the board for every reader who moves by keyboard. --}}
  {{#if showPages}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-pages">
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-pages-strip" role="tablist"
         aria-label="{{pagesLabel}}"
         {{#if pageOrderHint}}aria-describedby="{{pageOrderHintId}}"{{/if}}>{{{pageTabs}}}</div>
    {{!-- HOW THE BOARDS ARE PUT IN AN ORDER, for the readers a `cursor: grab` never reaches. Read
          out when the strip takes focus and invisible to everybody else, which is the only shape
          this can take: the row's whole job is to say WHICH BOARD IS UP, and a line of instructions
          printed across it would be competing with the answer. See `pageOrderHint`. --}}
    {{#if pageOrderHint}}
    <p id="{{pageOrderHintId}}" class="stonetop-relmap-said">{{pageOrderHint}}</p>
    {{/if}}

    {{!-- WHAT CAN BE DONE TO THE BOARDS, and all three are edits, so the whole lot is behind the
          gate. Outside the scrolling strip beside it: a map with a dozen pages scrolls its tabs
          sideways, and tools that scrolled away with them would be tools a reader has to go looking
          for.

          ⚠ GLYPH ONLY, AND THE HINT IS THE WHOLE OF THE LABEL. These three used to carry their
          words beside the icon ("New page", "Rename", "Delete page"), which put three more pieces
          of prose on the one row whose entire job is to say WHICH BOARD IS UP: the tab names and
          the tools competed for the same eye, and every letter of a tool was width the strip of
          tabs did not have. Every other row of small tools in the system is bare glyphs on a
          transparent square (the steading's wonder and encounter headers), and this is that shape.

          NOTHING IS LOST TO A READER WHO CANNOT READ THE GLYPH. The accessible name was never the
          span: `aria-label` overrode it then and carries the same full sentence now, which is the
          text `data-tooltip` shows on hover. The words are one hover, or one screen reader, away
          exactly as they were. --}}
    {{#if canEdit}}
    {{!-- ⚠ NEW PAGE SITS AT THE END OF THE TABS AND NOT WITH THE OTHER TWO, which is the one thing
          on this row that is placed by what it makes rather than by what it acts on. It makes
          ANOTHER TAB, and the place a new tab appears is after the last one — the same spot every
          tabbed thing a reader has ever used puts it, and near enough to the names that it reads as
          part of the strip rather than as a tool aimed at the board. Off at the far right it was
          the width of the whole window away from the row it adds to, and sat between two controls
          that do something else entirely.

          It is OUTSIDE the scrolling strip all the same: with a dozen boards the tabs scroll
          sideways, and a plus that scrolled off with them would be the only way to make a board at
          all, gone. So it stands just past the strip's right edge and stays there, which on a map
          whose tabs do not fill the row is exactly where the last tab ends. --}}
    <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-page-tool stonetop-relmap-page-tool--new"
            data-relmap-action="pagenew"
            data-tooltip="{{pageNewHint}}" aria-label="{{pageNewHint}}">
      <i class="fas fa-plus"></i>
    </button>

    {{!-- THE EYE, THE PEN AND THE TRASH ACT ON THE BOARD THAT IS UP, and they are pinned to the
          row's right-hand end rather than trailing the tabs: their target is the current tab, which
          is somewhere in a strip that moves, so a fixed corner is the only place they can be
          reliably reached for. Two of the three change what somebody else can see or destroy
          something outright, and standing them apart from the plus is what keeps a mis-aimed click
          on "another board" from being a click on "lose this one". --}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-pages-tools">
      {{!-- WHO AT THIS TABLE MAY LOOK AT THIS BOARD, and it is the first of the three because it is
            the only one whose state a GM reads rather than presses. Every board starts hidden from
            the players (module/relmap/relmap-doc.js): a board is a picture the GM is still working
            out, and one that arrived shared would give away who has not been met yet, who is lying
            to whom, and what is under the hill, the moment it had a face on it.

            ⚠ ALWAYS RENDERED AND HIDDEN, like the trash below and for the same reason: `_paintSeen`
            can only write onto markup a repaint left standing. Whether it applies at all is
            `pageHideOn` — a GM, on a map with a board under them — and that is written on every
            repaint rather than baked into this markup.

            THE GLYPH IS THE STATE AND THE HINT IS THE OUTCOME: an open eye on a board the table can
            see, a struck one on a board that is the GM's alone, and the sentence under the pointer
            says what pressing it would do. The hint is the whole of the label, as it is on the
            other three tools. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-page-tool stonetop-relmap-page-tool--seen
              {{~#if pageHidden}} is-hidden-board{{/if}}" data-relmap-action="pagehide"
              data-tooltip="{{pageHideHint}}" aria-label="{{pageHideHint}}"
              {{#unless pageHideOn}}hidden{{/unless}}>
        <i class="fas {{pageHideIcon}}"></i>
      </button>

      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-page-tool" data-relmap-action="pagerename"
              data-tooltip="{{pageRenameHint}}" aria-label="{{pageRenameHint}}">
        <i class="fas fa-pen"></i>
      </button>

      {{!-- ⚠ ALWAYS RENDERED AND HIDDEN, never behind an `{{#if}}`, for the reason the two panels
            over the board are: `_paintPages` can only write onto markup a repaint left standing,
            and whether the last board may be rubbed out changes whenever anybody at the table adds
            or removes one. Behind a condition this button would exist on precisely the maps that
            already had two pages when the window last rendered — so a reader who adds a second
            page would have no way to remove it again until they reopened the window. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-page-tool stonetop-relmap-page-tool--drop"
              data-relmap-action="pagedelete" data-tooltip="{{pageDeleteHint}}"
              aria-label="{{pageDeleteHint}}" {{#unless canDropPage}}hidden{{/unless}}>
        <i class="fas fa-trash"></i>
      </button>
    </div>
    {{/if}}
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{!-- The window the board moves behind: it clips, it does not scroll. A scroll container can only
        reach what is inside its own content box, so it would pin the board to its edges and grow
        scrollbars over a diagram that is meant to be dragged around freely.

        It is also the PANEL the strip above names, which is why it carries the tab roles' other
        half: a `role="tab"` with nothing saying what it controls is a tab in name only. The label
        is rewritten by `_paintPages`, because which tab is selected can change without a render —
        somebody else deleting the board this reader was standing on moves them to another. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-view" {{#if showPages}}role="tabpanel"
       aria-labelledby="{{pagePanelId}}"{{/if}}>
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-board">{{{board}}}</div>

    {{!-- Nobody on the map yet. Inside the viewport rather than instead of it, so the board is
          still there to be dropped onto — which is the other way somebody arrives.

          ALWAYS RENDERED, hidden rather than absent, because `_toggleEmpty` can only show and hide
          what the markup already has. Behind an `{{#if}}` it existed on exactly the maps that
          opened empty, so removing the last person from a populated one left a blank board with no
          lead text and no way in — the moment the panel is most wanted. The button inside it is
          still gated on permission, and the window re-renders whole either way.

          ⚠ THE PANEL MUST KEEP EXACTLY ONE CALL TO ACTION. This panel takes no pointer events (the
          board underneath it has to stay droppable), so its button opts back in — and it is the
          only thing inside the viewport a reader can tab to on a brand new map. It used to be "Add
          everyone", which put the whole cast on in one press; that button is gone, because a board
          with everybody on it is exactly the crowding a second page exists to escape. What replaced
          it is "Add someone", which is what the hint beside it already told the reader to use. --}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-empty" {{#unless empty}}hidden{{/unless}}>
      <p class="stonetop-relmap-empty-lead">{{emptyLead}}</p>
      <p class="stonetop-relmap-empty-hint">{{emptyHint}}</p>
      {{!-- ALWAYS RENDERED AND HIDDEN: the window can only WRITE onto markup a repaint left
            standing, and whether there is a button here at all depends on whether the reader may
            edit — which can change without the render that would otherwise have to put it there. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-cta stonetop-relmap-empty-cast"
              data-relmap-action="{{emptyAction.action}}" {{#unless emptyAction}}hidden{{/unless}}>
        <i class="fas {{emptyAction.icon}}"></i>
        <span>{{emptyAction.label}}</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    {{!-- THE LINE THE READER HAS HOLD OF, and the four things they change about it most.

          WHY IT IS NOT THE DIALOG. Clicking a line used to open a modal window asking six
          questions, and a window that has to be opened, aimed at and dismissed for each of them is
          six windows between one thought and the next. What a line SAYS, what colour it is, which
          way it is read and whether it is broken are the four a table changes while talking, and
          they are all here. Rubbing the line out is the fifth, and it is the last button on this
          bar. The sixth, what family tie a line is, is gone with the family tree it drew.

          THE WRITING GOES ONTO THE LINE, AND THERE IS NO BOX ON THE BAR ANY MORE. A caption is
          words drawn along a stroke, and a reader writing one used to be watching a text field on a
          strip of chrome instead -- reading their sentence in one place while the thing it belonged
          to sat underneath still saying something else, and finding out how it really looked only
          once they had stopped. The field below is still a real `input`, because nothing else gives
          an IME, a caret, a selection, paste or a screen reader anything to work with -- but it is
          off the bar (`.stonetop-relmap-tiebar-words` clips it to a pixel) and every keystroke is
          painted straight onto the line by `_sayLine`. It still takes the focus on opening, which
          is the whole of "click a line and start typing"; everything else on the bar is a press,
          and the bar's keydown hands a printable key back to this field from whichever press the
          reader last made.

          ⚠ INSIDE THE VIEWPORT AND OUTSIDE THE BOARD, which is the same choice the empty panel above
          makes and matters more here. The board's markup is REPLACED on every live update
          (`_repaintBoard` writes its innerHTML), so a bar that rode on the board would be destroyed
          under the reader's hands the moment anybody else at the table moved a portrait -- taking a
          half-typed caption and the focus with it. Out here it is only re-placed, by `place()`.

          ⚠ AND IT IS `hidden` RATHER THAN ABSENT, for the reason every panel in this window is:
          `RelmapTieBar` can only write onto markup a render left standing. --}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar" role="group" aria-label="{{tie.label}}" hidden>
      {{!-- ⚠ FIRST IN THE MARKUP AND INVISIBLE ON THE BAR. First because that is where the focus
            lands and where a tab reaches it; invisible because what it holds is drawn on the line.
            No `placeholder` and no `list`: neither could be seen, and what a line with nothing
            written on it looks like is a bare stretch of stroke, which is the truth. --}}
      <input type="text" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-words" data-relmap-tie="words"
             maxlength="{{maxLength}}" aria-label="{{labelField}}"
             autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">

      {{!-- THE COLOUR, AS THE ANSWER ON THE BAR AND A PALETTE UNDER IT.

            IT WAS EIGHT DISCS IN A ROW, AND THEN A NAMED CHOOSER, AND IT IS NEITHER NOW. Eight
            26px discs was a third of a strip that floats over the drawing, spent on one of its four
            questions. A `select` gave that back and named all eight -- and cost 10.4em of the same
            bar, wide enough for its longest entry, standing there all the time so that a question
            nobody is asking went on covering the board. It cost the one press as well: open, find,
            release, for a colour that used to be one click.

            SO THE QUESTION IS ASKED WHERE THERE IS ROOM FOR IT. What stands on the bar is the
            ANSWER -- one disc and one word, "Rose" -- and pressing it drops a palette underneath
            with the colours laid out to be aimed at. One press to open it and one per colour after
            that, and the palette is over the board only while somebody is actually choosing.

            AND THE PALETTE IS A GRID OF DISCS, EIGHT ACROSS, which is what a table asked for and
            what forty more colours made necessary. It was two columns with each colour's name
            printed beside its disc; the same layout would now be eighty rows of text.

            ⚠ AND THE COLOUR IS STILL NEVER THE ONLY THING SAID. Every swatch carries its NAME, in
            the tooltip and in its spoken label, and the bar goes on saying in words which colour the
            line is at all times -- so a reader who cannot resolve two colours against each other,
            and there are two at this table who cannot, still chooses by reading. What is gone is the
            word printed forty-eight times over, which is not the same thing. That promise is the
            same one the dash patterns make on the board itself under the high-contrast skin, and it
            is the one thing the eight-discs-in-a-row version could not keep at all. --}}
      <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-group stonetop-relmap-tiebar-inks">
        {{!-- THE ANSWER AS IT STANDS, AND THE WAY IN. The ink class and the word are both written
              by `RelmapTieBar` on open and on every pick: `slate` here only so the first paint is a
              colour rather than a hole, and the word empty because a render knows nothing about a
              line the reader has not clicked yet.

              ⚠ `data-relmap-said` IS THE PATTERN ITS SPOKEN NAME IS BUILT FROM. The bar is a
              DOM component with no globals and no i18n in it, so the words come in from the render
              -- the same way the arrow buttons get theirs, and for the same reason. --}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-inkopen" data-relmap-tie="inkopen"
                aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="{{inkLabel}}"
                data-relmap-said="{{inkNamed}}" data-tooltip="{{inkLabel}}">
          <span class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-inkmark stonetop-relmap-ink--slate"
                data-relmap-tie-mark="ink" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="stonetop-relmap-ink-swatch"></span>
          </span>
          <span class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-inkword" data-relmap-tie-name="ink"
                aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <i class="fas fa-caret-down" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </button>

        {{!-- THE PALETTE.

              ⚠ A CHILD OF THE BAR, WHICH IS WHAT KEEPS EVERY SWATCH IN IT CLICKABLE. `BOARD_CONTROLS`
              in the window names `.stonetop-relmap-tiebar` as a thing a press must not start a pan
              from; a panel opened OUTSIDE it would have its mousedown taken as the start of a pan,
              and the pointer capture that takes retargets the click at the viewport -- so every
              swatch in here would be dead on a dead-centre click that never moved a pixel.

              ⚠ AND RENDERED ALWAYS AND HIDDEN, like every other panel over this board, because
              `RelmapTieBar` can only write onto markup a render left standing. --}}
        <div class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop" data-relmap-tie-pop="ink" hidden>
          {{!-- ONE RADIO GROUP OVER BOTH ROWS. The eight and the colours already on the board are
                one question with one answer -- a line is drawn in exactly one colour -- so they are
                one group, with one tab stop and the arrow keys running through the whole of it. The
                `+` and the picker below are OUTSIDE it: they ask a second question rather than
                answering this one. --}}
          {{!-- ⚠ `data-relmap-ink-across` IS THE ARROW KEYS AND NOT A STYLE HOOK. Up and Down in
                here step by a ROW, and this is what a row is; `_across` in utils/relmap-tie-bar.js
                reads it off whichever button is focused, which is why it sits on the block that
                holds all three grids rather than on any one of them. It has to be the width the
                stylesheet actually paints -- see `RELMAP_INK_ACROSS` in relmap/relmap-ink.js, and
                the test that holds the two together. --}}
          <div class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-choices" role="radiogroup" aria-label="{{inkLabel}}"
               data-relmap-ink-across="{{inkAcross}}">
            {{!-- THE EIGHT THIS SYSTEM SHIPS, and they stay at the head of the palette because they
                  are the ones that can do what a hex cannot: each is retuned under the high-contrast
                  skin and each carries a dash pattern of its own, so a reader who cannot resolve two
                  of them apart still has the stroke itself telling them which is which.

                  ⚠ THE NAME IS OFF THE SWATCH AND NOT OUT OF THE CONTROL. It was printed beside
                  every disc, which is what made this two columns wide; a grid of colours is what the
                  table asked for and a grid is what reads as a palette. But the word was never
                  decoration -- there are readers at this table who choose by reading -- so it is
                  still on every swatch, in the tooltip and in the spoken label, and the TRIGGER goes
                  on saying the colour of the open line in words at all times. What is gone is the
                  word repeated forty times over; what a reader can still do is rest on a swatch, or
                  arrow onto it, and be told which colour it is.

                  `data-relmap-name` is how the bar puts that name on the trigger without
                  knowing a word of English: it reads it off whichever swatch is set. --}}
            <div class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-grid">
              {{#each inks}}
              <button type="button"
                      class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-swatch stonetop-relmap-ink--{{key}}"
                      data-relmap-tie="ink" data-relmap-tie-value="{{key}}"
                      data-relmap-name="{{name}}" role="radio" aria-checked="false"
                      tabindex="-1" aria-label="{{name}}" data-tooltip="{{name}}">
                <span class="stonetop-relmap-ink-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              </button>
              {{/each}}
            </div>

            {{!-- THE REST OF THE WHEEL. Forty colours nobody named, offered by the handful rather
                  than one at a time through the system colour dialog. See `RELMAP_INK_PRESETS` in
                  relmap/relmap-ink.js, which says what each one costs against the eight above and
                  why every one of them already clears the floor a line has to clear.

                  ⚠ PRINTED FROM THE MODULE, NOT WRITTEN OUT HERE. A second copy of forty hexes is a
                  second copy to drift, and the one that would drift silently is this one -- the
                  swatch would go on showing a colour the board no longer draws.

                  ⚠ AND THE COLOUR RIDES AS `--relmap-ink-raw` UNDER THE `--custom` CLASS, which is
                  exactly how a line of this colour is painted on the board. Written as `--relmap-ink`
                  instead, the swatch would sit at the raw colour under the high-contrast skin while
                  the line it draws was taken 28% darker -- a palette lying about the board in the
                  one skin whose whole job is to be legible.

                  ⚠ AND EVERY ONE OF THEM IS NAMED, in the tooltip and in the spoken label, for the
                  same reason the eight above are: the words came off the discs, they did not stop
                  mattering. `data-relmap-name` is read by the bar to put that name on the
                  trigger, so a line drawn in one of these says "Deep jade" rather than a hex. --}}
            <div class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-grid stonetop-relmap-inkpop-grid--more">
              {{#each inkPresets}}
              <button type="button"
                      class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-swatch stonetop-relmap-ink--custom"
                      data-relmap-tie="ink" data-relmap-tie-value="{{hex}}"
                      data-relmap-name="{{name}}" data-relmap-ink-preset="{{hex}}"
                      style="--relmap-ink-raw:{{hex}}"
                      role="radio" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1"
                      aria-label="{{name}}" data-tooltip="{{name}}">
                <span class="stonetop-relmap-ink-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              </button>
              {{/each}}
            </div>

            {{!-- THE COLOURS ALREADY ON THIS BOARD, which is what "your own colours" means here.
                  Not a list remembered per reader: a board drawn in one table's colours shows that
                  table its colours, on every client at it, with nothing stored anywhere to say so.
                  The window reads them off the graph and `RelmapTieBar` paints them in.

                  ⚠ FIXED SLOTS, RENDERED AND HIDDEN, rather than elements built when they are
                  wanted -- the house rule for everything over this board: the bar writes onto
                  markup a render left standing. The whole block goes away on a board with no
                  colours of its own on it, so a heading over an empty row never appears. --}}
            <div class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-mine" data-relmap-tie-mine="ink" hidden>
              <span class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-heading">{{inkCustomHeading}}</span>
              <div class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-row">
                {{#each inkCustomSlots}}
                <button type="button"
                        class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-swatch stonetop-relmap-ink--custom"
                        data-relmap-tie="ink" data-relmap-tie-value=""
                        data-relmap-ink-slot="{{this}}" data-relmap-said="{{../inkCustomNamed}}"
                        role="radio" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1" hidden>
                  <span class="stonetop-relmap-ink-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </button>
                {{/each}}
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          {{!-- A COLOUR OF THE READER'S OWN. Foundry's own element (`HTMLColorPickerElement`): a hex
                field and a swatch that opens the system colour dialog, so it is the control this
                table already knows from every other window in Foundry.

                ⚠ IT OPENS ON THE COLOUR THE LINE IS ALREADY WEARING and writes nothing by itself.
                The `+` seeds it with that -- rose, if the line is rose -- so the reader starts from
                where they are rather than from whatever black the element came up holding, and the
                line keeps its colour until they actually choose another.

                ⚠ RENDERED ALWAYS AND HIDDEN, and unlike the rest of the panels over this board this
                one is a CUSTOM ELEMENT, which has to have been connected to the document to have
                built its own two inputs at all. Left out until it was wanted, it would arrive empty.

                ⚠ AND WHAT COMES OUT OF IT IS NOT WHAT GOES ON THE BOARD. A colour under 3:1 against
                the paper is a line the reader on the magnifier cannot follow, so a pale one is
                deepened along its own hue before it is written, and the bar says it has done it.
                See `deepenInk` in relmap/relmap-ink.js. --}}
          <div class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-more">
            <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-inkpop-add" data-relmap-tie="inkmore">
              <i class="fas fa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i>
              <span>{{inkCustomField}}</span>
            </button>
            <color-picker class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-hex" data-relmap-tie="inkhex"
                          aria-label="{{inkCustomField}}" data-tooltip="{{inkCustomField}}"
                          value="" hidden></color-picker>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {{!-- WHICH WAY IT IS READ. Four presses and not three, because "no arrow at either end" is
            an answer as much as the other three are, and is the one most lines want.

            ⚠ THE TWO ONE-WAY BUTTONS ARE NOT LEFT AND RIGHT. Which of the pair is drawn on the left
            of the board is a fact about where two portraits happen to sit, and it changes when
            either of them is dragged; a fixed left arrow would mean "towards Sonya" on one board and
            "towards Mike" on the same board a moment later. So the icons and the words are BOTH
            written on open, by `RelmapTieBar`, from where the two faces actually are. --}}
      <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-group stonetop-relmap-tiebar-dirs" role="radiogroup"
           aria-label="{{dirLabel}}">
        {{#each dirs}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-btn" data-relmap-tie="dir"
                data-relmap-tie-value="{{key}}" role="radio" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1"
                data-tooltip="{{name}}" aria-label="{{name}}">
          <i class="fas {{icon}}"></i>
        </button>
        {{/each}}
      </div>

      {{!-- WHETHER THE STROKE IS BROKEN, AND HOW. A mark the reader makes on purpose -- the rumour
            against the fact, the tie that was against the tie that is -- and not the same thing as
            the dash an ink wears under the high-contrast skin. See RELMAP_DASHES in
            relmap/relmap-store.js, which says what that costs and why there are three of them.

            IT WAS A ROW OF PRESSES AND IT IS A CHOOSER NOW, FOR THE REASON THE COLOUR IS ONE. Two
            26px squares was already a sixth of a strip that floats over the drawing, spent on the
            question a table asks least of the four; a third answer would have made it a quarter,
            standing open all evening. So what stands on the bar is the ANSWER -- the stroke this
            line is actually drawn with -- and the three are laid out under it when it is pressed.
            One press to open and one per stroke after that, and a fourth kind costs no width at all.

            ⚠ AND THE ANSWER ON THE BAR IS A DRAWN RULE, WITH THE NAME IN WORDS UNDERNEATH IT. The
            colour's trigger prints its name beside its disc because a colour cannot be read by
            anybody who cannot resolve it; a stroke IS its shape, and the shape is the same one the
            line is drawn with, so the sample carries itself. The words are still there -- on the
            trigger's spoken label and its tooltip, and beside every rule in the panel -- because a
            18px sample is small, and reading is how the reader on the magnifier chooses. --}}
      <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-group stonetop-relmap-tiebar-dashes">
        {{!-- THE ANSWER AS IT STANDS, AND THE WAY IN. Which rule is drawn here, and what the
              trigger is called, are both written by `RelmapTieBar` on open and on every pick:
              `solid` here only so the first paint is a line rather than a hole. --}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-dashopen" data-relmap-tie="dashopen"
                aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="{{dashLabel}}"
                data-relmap-said="{{dashNamed}}" data-tooltip="{{dashLabel}}">
          <span class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-rule stonetop-relmap-tiebar-rule--solid"
                data-relmap-tie-mark="dash" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <i class="fas fa-caret-down" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </button>

        {{!-- THE THREE STROKES.

              ⚠ A CHILD OF THE BAR, like the palette above and for the same two reasons:
              `BOARD_CONTROLS` in the window names `.stonetop-relmap-tiebar` as a thing a press must
              not start a pan from, and a panel opened outside it would have every row in it killed
              by the pointer capture that starts one -- and `RelmapTieBar` can only write onto markup
              a render left standing, so it is rendered always and `hidden`.

              A LIST AND NOT A GRID, which is why there is no `data-relmap-ink-across` on it: a row
              here is one button, and `_across` answering one is exactly the stride this wants. --}}
        <div class="stonetop-relmap-dashpop" data-relmap-tie-pop="dash" hidden>
          <div class="stonetop-relmap-dashpop-choices" role="radiogroup" aria-label="{{dashLabel}}">
            {{#each dashes}}
            {{!-- ⚠ A DRAWN RULE AND NOT AN ICON. This was Font Awesome's `fa-ellipsis` for dotted,
                  which is three dots in a row -- sitting two buttons away from `fa-ellipsis-vertical`,
                  which is the "everything else" button. Two ellipses side by side on a strip this
                  small read as one control, and the busier of them at 13px reads as broken type
                  rather than as a line (the same failure the pin glyphs hit at that size). What each
                  of these means is a LINE, so each one draws its own.

                  `data-relmap-name` is how the bar puts this stroke's name on the trigger
                  without a word of i18n in it: it reads it off whichever row is set, exactly as it
                  reads a colour's name off its swatch. --}}
            <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-dashpop-row"
                    data-relmap-tie="dash" data-relmap-tie-value="{{key}}"
                    data-relmap-name="{{name}}"
                    role="radio" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1"
                    data-tooltip="{{name}}" aria-label="{{name}}">
              <span class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-rule stonetop-relmap-tiebar-rule--{{key}}"
                    aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="stonetop-relmap-dashpop-word" aria-hidden="true">{{name}}</span>
            </button>
            {{/each}}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {{!-- HOW BIG THE WRITING ON THIS LINE IS SET, as a number on the bar and a list under it.

            WHY A LINE HAS THIS AT ALL. Everything else on this bar is what the line MEANS; this is
            how loudly it says it. A board of forty people is read zoomed in, and the two lines on
            it a table actually cares about sit in the same twelve-pixel whisper as the thirty-eight
            that only say who lives next door. It is the same kind of mark as the colour, and it is
            stored on the line, so everybody at the table sees the board the reader arranged.

            ⚠ THE NUMBER IS THE ANSWER ON THE BAR, which is the third arrangement of the three
            choosers beside it and the right one here. A colour shows a disc AND its name, because a
            colour cannot be read by somebody who cannot resolve it. A stroke shows the rule itself,
            because a stroke IS its shape. A size has a name for the five steps offered and NO name
            at all for one somebody typed, so the honest answer that is always there is the number.
            The words are still said on the trigger's spoken label and its tooltip.

            AND THE STEPS SHOW THEMSELVES. Each row in the panel is its own word set at its own
            size, which is what a reader is actually choosing between; a column of five identical
            labels would make them read the numbers to find out what the list meant.

            ⚠ THE INLINE SIZE IN THAT SAMPLE IS THE DATUM AND NOT A STYLE. What each step IS is a
            number, printed here from the one list that holds them (`RELMAP_SIZES`). A class per
            step would mean writing those numbers again in the stylesheet, where nothing would
            notice the two lists drifting apart. --}}
      <div class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-group stonetop-relmap-tiebar-sizes">
        {{!-- THE ANSWER AS IT STANDS, AND THE WAY IN. The number here and what the trigger is
              called are both written by `RelmapTieBar` on open and on every pick: the ordinary
              size is printed only so the first paint is a number rather than a hole. --}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-sizeopen" data-relmap-tie="sizeopen"
                aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="{{sizeLabel}}"
                data-relmap-said="{{sizeNamed}}" data-tooltip="{{sizeLabel}}">
          <i class="fas fa-text-height" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <span class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-sizeword" data-relmap-tie-mark="size"
                aria-hidden="true">{{sizeBase}}</span>
          <i class="fas fa-caret-down" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </button>

        {{!-- THE STEPS, AND A FIELD FOR A SIZE NOBODY OFFERED.

              ⚠ A CHILD OF THE BAR, like the two panels above and for the same two reasons:
              `BOARD_CONTROLS` in the window names `.stonetop-relmap-tiebar` as a thing a press must
              not start a pan from, and a panel opened outside it would have every row in it killed
              by the pointer capture that starts one -- and `RelmapTieBar` can only write onto markup
              a render left standing, so it is rendered always and `hidden`.

              A LIST AND NOT A GRID, so no `data-relmap-ink-across`: a row here is one button, and
              `_across` answering one is exactly the stride the arrow keys want. --}}
        <div class="stonetop-relmap-sizepop" data-relmap-tie-pop="size" hidden>
          <div class="stonetop-relmap-sizepop-choices" role="radiogroup" aria-label="{{sizeLabel}}">
            {{#each sizes}}
            {{!-- `data-relmap-name` is how the bar puts this step's name on the trigger without a
                  word of i18n in it, exactly as the strokes and the swatches above do. --}}
            <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-sizepop-row"
                    data-relmap-tie="size" data-relmap-tie-value="{{px}}"
                    data-relmap-name="{{name}}"
                    role="radio" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1"
                    data-tooltip="{{name}}" aria-label="{{name}}">
              <span class="stonetop-relmap-sizepop-sample" style="font-size: {{px}}px"
                    aria-hidden="true">{{name}}</span>
            </button>
            {{/each}}
          </div>

          {{!-- A SIZE OF THEIR OWN, WHICH IS THE NINTH COLOUR AGAIN. Five steps are what a table
                reaches for; the reader on the magnifier wants the one number that works on their
                screen, and a list they cannot add to is a list that stops short of them.

                ⚠ AND IT IS SHOWN RATHER THAN HIDDEN BEHIND A PRESS, which the colour picker above
                is not. That one is a whole picker with a swatch, a hex field and a system dialog
                behind it, and it opens for a colour nobody named; this is one small number field,
                and a press to reach it would be a press between a reader and the only control on
                this bar that can give them a size that suits their eyes.

                `min` and `max` are the bounds `readSize` will actually keep, said to the browser
                as well so the spinner cannot walk out of them. What is TYPED is still held to
                them on the way through: an input's `min` is not a promise. --}}
          <label class="stonetop-relmap-sizepop-own">
            <span class="stonetop-relmap-sizepop-ownword">{{sizeOwnLabel}}</span>
            <input type="number" class="stonetop-relmap-sizepop-num" data-relmap-tie="sizenum"
                   min="{{sizeMin}}" max="{{sizeMax}}" step="1" inputmode="numeric"
                   autocomplete="off" aria-label="{{sizeOwnField}}">
          </label>
        </div>
      </div>

      {{!-- DELETING THE LINE, and it asks nothing first. Every other destructive gesture in
            this window confirms, and this one deliberately does not: the bar is the surface a
            reader clicks around on while talking, a confirm on it is the modal this bar exists to
            be rid of, and one press back on the undo puts the line and everything it said straight
            back. Set apart from the presses beside it by a rule, because those change a line and
            this one ends it. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tiebar-btn stonetop-relmap-tiebar-rub"
              data-relmap-tie="rub" data-tooltip="{{tie.rub}}" aria-label="{{tie.rub}}">
        <i class="fas fa-trash"></i>
      </button>
    </div>

    {{!-- OPEN THIS BOARD IN A WINDOW OF ITS OWN, in the corner of the board itself.

          ONLY ON A SURFACE THAT HAS SOMEWHERE TO POP OUT FROM, which today means the steading
          sheet's Relationship Map tab (module/dialogs/RelationshipMapPanel.js sets `canPopOut`).
          The window never renders it: a window offering to open itself would be a press that
          brings the reader back to where they already are.

          THE SAME PLACE AND THE SAME MARK AS THE EXPEDITION'S MAP (user, 2026-09-07), which has
          had a way out to a bigger view since it was built: a small tile in the picture's top
          right, fading in under the pointer. A reader who wants more room already has their hand
          over the thing they want more of, so that is where the way out belongs. On the footer
          row -- where this button first went -- it was a labelled control among the ones that
          write to the shared board, which is a different kind of thing.

          NOT AN EDIT, so it is outside the `canEdit` gate the tools below sit behind. The reader
          who wants it most is the one who may only LOOK: they cannot move a portrait to see what
          is behind it, so more room is the only thing they have.

          ⚠ IT IS INSIDE THE VIEWPORT, so it lives or dies by `BOARD_CONTROLS`. A press the pan
          surface does not recognise takes a pointer capture, the capture retargets the click at
          the viewport, and the button is dead on a dead-centre press that never moved a pixel.
          `[data-relmap-action]` is already named in that list, which is why this needs no entry of
          its own -- and why it must keep that attribute rather than growing one of its own. --}}
    {{#if canPopOut}}
    <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool stonetop-relmap-popout"
            data-relmap-action="popout"
            data-tooltip="{{popOutLabel}}" aria-label="{{popOutLabel}}">
      <i class="fas fa-expand"></i>
    </button>
    {{/if}}
  </div>

  {{!-- THE FOOTER: WHAT THIS READER SEES, AND WHAT THEY CAN DO.

        EVERYTHING PRESSED AT THIS WINDOW IS ON THIS ONE LINE, and it is under the board rather
        than over it. The toolbar this replaces stood between the window's title and the page
        strip, which put three buttons in the path of the two things a reader actually opened the
        map for: which board is up, and the board. Down here nothing is in that path, the board
        gains the height the bar was spending, and the controls are still in one place — a corner
        the eye returns to, and the one where a window's own settings live everywhere else in this
        system. (The resize grip is in the OTHER corner, which is why the box below sits left and
        the buttons sit right.)

        LEFT: WHAT IS DRAWN, FOR THIS READER. Right: what changes the board, and what takes a
        change back. The two halves are the two kinds of thing here — a filter that belongs to one
        person and writes nothing, against edits that everyone at the table sees — and the width of
        the window between them says so without a rule.

        THE WORDS ALONG THE LINES, drawn or left off. A filter on what is drawn, belonging to one
        reader, and the sort of thing looked for once an evening and then left alone.

        ⚠ ALWAYS OFFERED. It stands in the place a narrower box used to — one that put away the
        lines an old import had written, and appeared only on the boards carrying any — and this
        one is about a board of forty people rather than about one board's history, so there is no
        condition to render it behind. Nothing here is destroyed either way, which is why it is
        the one control in this window a reader who may only look can still press.

        The tooltip is on the LABEL, so resting on either the box or its words explains why anybody
        would want this — which is not obvious from two words, and is the whole reason the option
        exists. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-foot">
    <label class="stonetop-relmap-foot-check" data-tooltip="{{labelsHint}}">
      <input type="checkbox" class="stonetop-check" data-relmap-action="hidelabels"
             {{#if hideLabels}}checked{{/if}}>
      <span>{{labelsLabel}}</span>
    </label>

    {{!-- HOW HEAVILY THIS BOARD IS DRAWN, FOR THIS READER: the arrowheads, the strokes and the
          writing, each as a percentage of what the stylesheet sets.

          WHY THERE ARE THREE AND NOT A ZOOM. The board already zooms, and zooming answers a
          different question -- it magnifies everything at once, so a web too dense to read is still
          too dense and there is simply less of it in the window. What a reader runs out of is one
          THING at a time: an arrowhead is a speck at the end of a line on a board of forty, a
          stroke is three and a half screen pixels, and the writing is sixteen. Each of these moves
          one of those and nothing else.

          ⚠ IT IS THIS READER'S OWN AND IT WRITES NOTHING TO THE MAP. Everything else pressed at
          this window edits a board everybody shares; this edits a record in one browser, flat
          across every world (module/relmap/relmap-weights.js says at length why it is not stored on
          the board). So it is outside the `canEdit` gate below, like the captions box beside it:
          the reader who needs it most is the one who may only look, because they cannot move a
          portrait to see anything better.

          ⚠ NEXT TO THE CAPTIONS BOX, WHICH IS THIS FOOTER'S OWN ARRANGEMENT rather than a place
          found for it. The line reads LEFT: WHAT IS DRAWN, FOR THIS READER; right: what changes the
          board for everybody. These four dials -- the box and the three weights -- are all the
          first kind, they all write to one browser and nobody else's window moves, so they stand
          together on the left and the auto margin on the tools keeps the other kind at the far end.

          ⚠ AND OUT OF THE VIEWPORT ALTOGETHER (user, 2026-09-09). It stood in the board's top-left
          corner as a stack of three rows, quiet until the pointer arrived. Down here it is
          permanent, it is in the one place this window puts everything that is pressed, it takes no
          board away from the diagram, and it is out of the pan surface -- which is three
          arrangements it no longer has to make: no hover reveal to find, no `z-index` against the
          tie bar, and no dependence on `BOARD_CONTROLS` to survive a pointer capture. The
          `data-relmap-action` attributes stay because that is what the click handler dispatches on.

          ⚠ ONE HINT ON EACH DIAL AND A NAME ON EVERY BUTTON. Three tooltips each explaining that
          this belongs to the reader alone would be the same paragraph three times over on one line.
          What a dial has to say for itself is which of the three it is. --}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-heft" role="group" aria-label="{{weightGroup}}">
      {{#each weights}}
      <div class="stonetop-relmap-heft-dial" data-tooltip="{{hint}}">
        <i class="stonetop-relmap-heft-mark {{icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        {{!-- LIGHTER ON THE LEFT AND HEAVIER ON THE RIGHT, with the number between them, because
              that is the order the value itself runs in and the order every stepper a reader has
              met is in. Dead at the ends rather than pressing to no effect. --}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-heft-step" data-relmap-action="weight"
                data-relmap-weight="{{key}}" data-relmap-step="-1"
                aria-label="{{down}}" {{#if atMin}}disabled{{/if}}>
          <i class="fas fa-minus" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </button>
        {{!-- THE READOUT IS ALSO THE WAY BACK to a hundred per cent, which is what its own label
              says: a third button per dial would be twelve controls on a line that has room for
              nine, and "put this one back" is the same question about the same one of the three.
              `aria-disabled` and not `disabled` at the base, so a screen reader still meets the
              number -- some skip a disabled button entirely, and the number is the point. --}}
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-heft-now" data-relmap-action="weight"
                data-relmap-weight="{{key}}" data-relmap-step="0"
                data-tooltip="{{reset}}" aria-label="{{now}}"
                aria-disabled="{{#if atBase}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}">{{pct}}%</button>
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-heft-step" data-relmap-action="weight"
                data-relmap-weight="{{key}}" data-relmap-step="1"
                aria-label="{{up}}" {{#if atMax}}disabled{{/if}}>
          <i class="fas fa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </button>
      </div>
      {{/each}}
    </div>

    {{!-- THE BOARD'S OWN TOOLS, behind the editing gate and nothing else, held against the
          right-hand edge by an auto margin on the group (`.stonetop-relmap-foot-tools`) rather
          than by a spacer.

          ⚠ NO "MATCH ANSWERS TO PEOPLE". A GM-only button stood first in this group and drew what
          the introductions had recorded onto the party's own board, for a table whose answers were
          taken down before the introductions thought to ask "who is this about?". It is gone: it
          was a button most tables never had anything for, on the one row every table looks at.

          ⚠ NO "BRING THE PARTY IN" AND NO "BRING THE VILLAGE IN" either. Both boards still seat
          themselves when the map is opened; what stood here was a pair of buttons asking for the
          same pass out loud, and the map already answers "somebody is missing from this board"
          twice over -- drag them on, or press "Add someone".

          ⚠ NO "TIDY UP". It laid the whole board out again, in a shape it asked for each time — a
          ring seated by who knows whom, or knots pulled together by their links — and it was the
          one tool here that threw away an arrangement the table had built up over a season in a
          single press. Where somebody is standing on a board is now only ever where somebody put
          them.

          ⚠ NO "RUB OUT PULLED-IN LINES". Two buttons stood here in turn. "Pull in ratings" wrote
          a line into this shared board for every rating anybody in the world had stored, one each
          way round, and a table that filled its sheets in during the introductions ended up with
          the handful of ties they drew by hand buried under a hundred they did not. What carries
          that information now is the board called "The Party", which seats the party by itself
          and draws what they actually answered about each other.

          The second button was the way out of what the first left behind, and it is gone too. A
          board somebody pressed the old import on is still carrying every line it made, and what
          a reader has for that now is the checkbox to the left: it puts those lines away for that
          reader alone, and rubs nothing out for anybody. One line at a time, the tie bar still
          rubs one out, with the undo button standing behind it.

          ⚠ NO CAPTIONS CYCLE. A three-way button stood here — every label, labels on hover, no
          labels — written onto the root as a class. Every caption is drawn now. What rescues a
          dense board instead is the lighting: resting on a face marks that person's whole web and
          the stylesheet quiets everything else, which is the question a crowded board is actually
          being asked. --}}
    {{#if canEdit}}
    <div class="stonetop-relmap-foot-tools">
      {{!-- TAKING A CHANGE BACK, AND PUTTING IT FORWARD AGAIN.

            BEFORE "ADD SOMEONE", AND SET APART FROM IT. These are the only two controls in the
            window that are about what the READER did rather than about the board, which is why
            they do not simply run on into the tool beside them: the button that follows carries
            the window's one filled fill, and that block of colour is the join.

            What they reverse is whatever this reader last did to this board, wherever they were
            standing when they did it, and each press says out loud what it took back — so unlike
            "Add someone", the effect is never invisible from where the reader is.

            ⚠ NO WORDS ON THEM, and nothing is lost by taking them off: the two curved arrows are
            the pair every program on the reader's machine draws these with, and what a press here
            actually says -- WHICH change it would take back, by name -- was never the word "Undo"
            on the button but its tooltip and its accessible name, both rewritten on every edit by
            `_paintHistory`. Without the words the pair reads as one small thing on the row rather
            than as two more tools. The `<i>` is now all that is inside a button, so
            the stylesheet takes core's icon-to-label margin back off it; see
            `.stonetop-relmap-history` for why a glyph-only button is otherwise off centre.

            RENDERED DISABLED AND SWITCHED ON BY `_paintHistory`, never behind an `{{#if}}`. What
            can be taken back changes on every edit, and buttons that appeared and vanished as the
            reader worked would shuffle the whole row sideways under the pointer. Their accessible
            name is written there too, and carries WHAT a press would take back — a reader who
            cannot see the board is exactly the one who needs telling before they press.

            ⚠ THE HISTORY IS THIS READER'S OWN and is never written to the map: see the head of
            module/relmap/relmap-history.js for why an undo here is a reversing write rather than a
            saved copy of the board, and why the table does not share one stack. --}}
      <span class="stonetop-relmap-history">
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="undo" disabled
                data-tooltip="{{undoNothing}}" aria-label="{{undoNothing}}">
          <i class="fas fa-rotate-left"></i>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool" data-relmap-action="redo" disabled
                data-tooltip="{{redoNothing}}" aria-label="{{redoNothing}}">
          <i class="fas fa-rotate-right"></i>
        </button>
      </span>

      {{!-- PUTTING SOMEBODY ON THE BOARD, and the last press on the row.

            LAST, AND FILLED. It is the only thing in this footer that adds to the board rather
            than changing what is already on it, and the only button in the window painted with
            the slate primary (`--st-btn-primary-*`, `.stonetop-relmap-add`) — which is what puts
            it at the end of the row: a filled button reads as the one thing to press, and a
            reader who has just opened an empty map should land on it after their eye has passed
            everything else rather than before.

            THE SAME PRESS THE EMPTY PANEL OFFERS, and deliberately not the same button. The panel
            over an empty board carries its own "Add someone" because a reader with nothing drawn
            yet should not have to find the footer; this one is the way back to the picker once
            there is a board to look at, and it stays whether the board is empty or full. --}}
      <button type="button" class="stonetop-relmap-tool stonetop-relmap-add"
              data-relmap-action="add"
              data-tooltip="{{addHint}}" aria-label="{{addHint}}">
        <i class="fas fa-user-plus"></i>
        <span>{{addLabel}}</span>
      </button>
    </div>
    {{/if}}
  </div>

  {{!-- Spoken to a screen reader when something moves, and deliberately OUTSIDE the board: the
        board's markup is replaced wholesale on every repaint, and a live region that goes with it
        is a live region nothing is ever announced from. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-relmap-live" aria-live="polite" role="status"></div>
</div>
